      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ea37be4f-ef2a-5ae8-923b-559739df898f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Temagami-Lorrain Mine, Burwash Mine, Northeastern Ontario, Temagami, Sauvé Lake, Cassels Township, Yellowknife Bay, Northwest Territories, Trail, British Columbia\n- Companies: Temagami-Lorrain Mining Company\n- People: Johnny Baker, Hugh Muir\n- Topics: gold mining, mine discovery, ore processing\n\nFacts:\n- The Temagami-Lorrain Mining Company worked on the Temagami-Lorrain Mine property in the early 1900s.\n- The Burwash Mine was discovered in the fall of 1934 by Johnny Baker and Hugh Muir at Yellowknife Bay in the Northwest Territories.\n- In 1935 and 1936, a short shaft was sunk at the Burwash Mine.\n- In the summer of 1935, a 16-ton bulk sample of ore from the Burwash Mine was shipped to Trail, British Columbia for processing and yielded 200 troy ounces (6 kg) of gold.\n- The Temagami-Lorrain Mine is an abandoned surface and underground mine in Northeastern Ontario, Canada.\n- The Temagami-Lorrain Mine is located about 10 km northeast of the town of Temagami near Sauvé Lake in central Cassels Township.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "fd429a39-bc55-5e65-bbd9-aef5b98da7a4",
        "text": "Page: Temagami-Lorrain Mine. Sentence: Temagami-Lorrain Mine is an abandoned surface and underground mine in Northeastern Ontario, Canada.\nPage: Temagami-Lorrain Mine. Sentence: It is located about 10 km northeast of the town of Temagami near Sauvé Lake in central Cassels Township.\nPage: Temagami-Lorrain Mine. Sentence: It is named after the Temagami-Lorrain Mining Company, which carried out work on the property in the early 1900s.\nPage: Burwash Mine. Sentence: The Burwash Mine was a small gold property discovered in the fall of 1934 by Johnny Baker and Hugh Muir at Yellowknife Bay, Northwest Territories.\nPage: Burwash Mine. Sentence: A short shaft was sunk in 1935-1936 at Burwash, and in the summer of 1935 a 16-ton bulk sample of ore was shipped to Trail, British Columbia for processing, yielding 200 troy ounces (6 kg) of gold.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
